<Project>

  <!--
    Solution-wide build settings, auto-imported by every project in the repo.

    x64-only. The app ships x64 exclusively (published as win-x64 / linux-x64), and the CefGlue native-interop
    assemblies the Linux/macOS head references are built for x64 (AMD64). Building AnyCPU (MSIL) tripped MSB3270
    (architecture mismatch) in "Any CPU" IDE builds. Targeting x64 outright makes the output architecture match
    those references — fixing the warning at the source rather than suppressing it — and matches how every
    artifact is actually published and run. Platforms drives the IDE's Configuration Manager (x64 only);
    PlatformTarget forces x64 output regardless of how the build is invoked (IDE, dotnet CLI, CI).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Platforms>x64</Platforms>
    <PlatformTarget>x64</PlatformTarget>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    CS8509 — "the switch expression does not handle all possible values of its input type" — is an
    ERROR here, not a warning.

    Warnings are not errors in this repo, so a non-exhaustive switch expression would build and ship,
    and then throw at whatever moment the unhandled member first turns up. That is the wrong trade
    here: the places that switch over a closed enum (an update-check status, an upload state) are
    exactly the places where a member added later must be routed deliberately, and a compile error is
    the cheapest possible moment to find that out.

    Note what this does NOT protect against - the alternative it replaced. A switch STATEMENT with a
    default arm compiles clean, never throws, and answers the new member with whatever the default
    said, which for the Help menu was "you are on the latest version". Silent and wrong beats loud
    and late; the expression form plus this promotion is what rules out both.

    Deliberately narrow. This promotes ONE diagnostic rather than turning on TreatWarningsAsErrors,
    which would make every future analyser release a build break.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CS8509</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
